I'll build each island as an elliptical disk using gmsh's occ.addDisk, then cut these islands out of the fused duct+loop shape right after fusing but before extrusion, so the boolean cut happens while everything is still a single 2D face.

If the islands sit fully inside the fused face without touching its outer boundary, the cut turns that face into a non-simply-connected region with a hole around each island—effectively the obstacle geometry I want. The island's own boundary then becomes a new wall patch that needs to be tagged separately, and I need to think through how that affects the meshing algorithm's topology handling.

This is fine since a face with holes still counts as one surface entity in the CAD kernel, so the "exactly one surface" assertion still holds after the cut. Then when I extract the boundary of that face, both the outer curves and the hole curves come back, and since the hole curves' bounding boxes won't align with the inlet/outlet x-extremes, my classification logic should naturally sort them into the "wall" category already.

I don't need to worry about winding orientation issues here since cutting a face by a fully interior tool is a robust operation, unlike the earlier fuse quirk with touching boundaries. Time to implement this.


